Thought: To determine the SMILES notation of the organic molecule, I should start by gathering some initial data about the molecule. The mass spectrometry spectra will provide the molecular weight and possible fragments, which can help in deducing the molecular formula. This is a crucial first step in identifying the structure of the molecule.
Action: mass_spectrometry_spectra
Action Input: {}

Thought: The mass spectrometry data indicates a molecular ion peak at m/z 224.12, which is likely the molecular weight of the compound. To deduce the molecular formula, I will use this information to obtain possible isomers. This will help in narrowing down the structure of the molecule.
Action: obtain_isomers_from_molecular_formula
Action Input: {"molecular_formula": "C14H16O3"}

Thought: I have obtained a list of possible isomers for the molecular formula C14H16O3. To further narrow down the correct structure, I should analyze the NMR spectra, starting with the proton NMR spectra, to identify the types of hydrogen environments present in the molecule.
Action: proton_nmr_spectra
Action Input: {}

Thought: Since the proton NMR prediction failed, I will proceed with analyzing the carbon NMR spectra. This can provide information about the carbon environments in the molecule, which will help in distinguishing between the possible isomers.
Action: carbon_nmr_spectra
Action Input: {}

Thought: With both proton and carbon NMR predictions failing, I should try using the IR spectra to identify functional groups present in the molecule. This can help in further narrowing down the possible isomers.
Action: ir_spectra
Action Input: {}

Thought: The IR spectra show a strong peak at 1723 cm⁻¹, which is indicative of a carbonyl group (C=O). The presence of peaks around 3100-3000 cm⁻¹ suggests aromatic C-H stretching, and peaks around 1600 cm⁻¹ suggest aromatic C=C stretching. This information can help in identifying the correct isomer from the list. I will now validate each SMILES string to see which one matches the IR data.
Action: validate_smiles
Action Input: {"smiles": "CC(C1=CC=C(C=C1)C(=O)O)C(=O)C2CCC2"}

Thought: The SMILES string "CC(C1=CC=C(C=C1)C(=O)O)C(=O)C2CCC2" is valid. Given the IR data indicating the presence of a carbonyl group and aromatic characteristics, this structure seems plausible. However, I should validate the other isomers as well to ensure this is the best match.
Action: validate_smiles
Action Input: {"smiles": "COC1=C(C=C(C=C1)C=O)O[C@@H]2CCCC=C2"}

Thought: The SMILES string "COC1=C(C=C(C=C1)C=O)O[C@@H]2CCCC=C2" is also valid. This structure contains a carbonyl group and aromatic features, which are consistent with the IR data. I will continue validating the remaining isomers to ensure a comprehensive analysis.
Action: validate_smiles
Action Input: {"smiles": "CC1CC(=O)C2=C(C1(C)C)C=CC(=C2)C(=O)O"}

Thought: The SMILES string "CC1CC(=O)C2=C(C1(C)C)C=CC(=C2)C(=O)O" is valid and also contains carbonyl and aromatic features. I will validate the remaining isomers to ensure all possibilities are considered.
Action: validate_smiles
Action Input: {"smiles": "COC(=O)C1C(CCCC1=O)C2=CC=CC=C2"}

Thought: The SMILES string "COC(=O)C1C(CCCC1=O)C2=CC=CC=C2" is valid and matches the IR data with carbonyl and aromatic features. I will validate the last isomer to complete the analysis.
Action: validate_smiles
Action Input: {"smiles": "CC(C)OC1=C(C=CC2=C1C=CCC2=O)OC"}
